n@misc{saengrungkongka2026extremaltintersectingfamiliespermutations, title={Extremal $t$-intersecting Families of Permutations for Large $t$}, author={Pitchayut Saengrungkongka}, year={2026}, eprint={2605.26051}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.26051}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEdge-regular graphs with non-negative curvature have... (https://arxivlens.com/paperview/details/edge-regular-graphs-with-non-negative-curvature-have-polynomial-growth-9266-7e1d1a68)\nciteturn3search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; Crawled: 4 weeks ago; A long-standing conjecture in the emerging discrete Bakry-Émery theory asserts that bounded-degree graphs satisfying $\\mathrm{CD}(0,\\infty)$ have polynomial growth. ... Paper ID: 2606.11094\n\nBack ArXiv math.CO Edge-regular graphs with non-negative curvature have polynomial growth\n\nArXiv math.CO math.PR\n\n# Edge-regular graphs with non-negative curvature have polynomial growth\n\nGuy Blachar, Hervé Pajot, Justin Salez\n\n2606.11094 Published Jun 9, 2026\n\n### Publication\n\nPublished: Jun 09, 2026\n\nCategories: math.CO, math.PR\n\n### Metrics\n\nSource: ArXiv\n\n### Paper Preview\n\n### Abstract\n\nA long-standing conjecture in the emerging discrete Bakry-Émery theory asserts that bounded-degree graphs satisfying $\\mathrm{CD}(0,\\infty)$ have polynomial growth. In the present paper, we prove this conjecture for all edge-regular graphs, and even obtain a volume doubling estimate with a constant that depends only on the degree. This is made possible thanks to the discovery of a surprising self-improvement phenomenon, which seems of independent interest: any edge-regular graph satisfying $\\mathrm{CD}(κ,\\infty)$ for some $κ\\in\\mathbb R$ must in fact satisfy $\\mathrm{CD}(κ,n)$ for some explicit, universal and optimal dimension parameter $n$.\n\n### Paper Details\n\nPaper ID: 2606.11094\n\nComments: 11 pages, 1 figure, comments welcome\n\nCategories:\n\nmath.CO math.PR\n\nView on arXiv Download PDF\n\n### Authors\n\nGB Guy Blachar 13 papers HP Hervé Pajot 17 papers JS Justin Salez 3 papers--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAhlswede–Khachatrian-type conjecture for t-intersecting families of permutations | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/374950/ahlswede-khachatrian-type-conjecture-for-t-intersecting-fami)\nciteturn3search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Ahlswede–Khachatrian-type conjecture for t-intersecting families of permutations ... This is the permutation analogue of the Ahlswede–Khachatrian theorem for $t$t-intersecting families of sets. ... Pitchayut Saengrungkongka, “Extremal t-intersecting Families of Permutations for Large t”, arXiv:2605.26051 (2026).\n\nAhlswede–Khachatrian-type conjecture for t-intersecting families of permutations\n\nLet $n$n and $t$t be positive integers, let $cSigma_n$c S i g m a n​ be the collection of permutations of $[n]$[n], and let $cA_k$c A k​ be the family\n\n$$cA_k=\\{\\sigma\\incSigma_n:\\sigma(i)=i\\text{ for at least }t+k\\text{ indices }i\\in[t+2k]\\}.$$c A k​={σ\\incSigma n​:σ(i)=i for at least t+k indices i∈[t+2 k]}.\n\nA family $\\calF\\subseteqcSigma_n$\\calF\\subseteqcSigma n​ is $t$t-intersecting if any two of its permutations agree on at least $t$t values of $i$i. Ahlswede–Khachatrian-type conjecture. If $\\calF\\subseteqcSigma_n$\\calF\\subseteqcSigma n​ is a $t$t-intersecting family, then\n\n$$|\\calF|\\leq\\max_k|\\calA_k|.$$∣\\calF∣≤k max​∣\\calA k​∣.\n\nMoreover, if equality holds, then $\\calF=\\sigma\\calA_k\\tau$\\calF=σ\\calA k​τ for some $k$k and permutations $\\sigma$σ and $\\tau$τ. This is the permutation analogue of the Ahlswede–Khachatrian theorem for $t$t-intersecting families of sets. The conjecture asks both for the sharp maximum size and for a classification of all extremal families; its resolution is not specified in the supplied source.\n\n## Primary source\n\nP